# 🔑 Key Takeaways

1. CI/CD pipelines automate software delivery, but they can become attack targets.
2. Secure pipelines require protected workflows, isolated runners, restricted permissions, and audit logging.
3. Secrets must never be hardcoded into source code or exposed in pipeline logs.
4. Environment variables help separate credentials from source code, but dedicated secrets managers provide stronger controls.
5. Dependency security protects applications from vulnerable and malicious third-party packages.
6. SCA tools identify known dependency vulnerabilities.
7. SBOMs help organizations understand which components are present in their software.
8. Build security protects the environment that produces software artifacts.
9. Cryptographic hashes help detect changes, while digital signatures help establish authenticity.
10. Deployment security protects production environments, credentials, infrastructure, and application configuration.
11. Security checks should be integrated into CI/CD pipelines.
12. A secure pipeline must protect the software delivery process from source code to production.

