# 🎯 Active Recall — Test Your Understanding

Try answering these questions without referring to the notes.

<details>
<summary><strong>1. What is CI/CD security?</strong></summary>

CI/CD security is the practice of protecting software development and delivery pipelines from vulnerabilities, unauthorized access, compromised dependencies, malicious modifications, and insecure deployments.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>2. Why can a CI/CD pipeline become an attractive target for attackers?</strong></summary>

Because pipelines may have access to source code, deployment credentials, cloud infrastructure, production environments, and software signing keys. Compromising a pipeline may allow attackers to manipulate software before it reaches users.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>3. What is a CI/CD runner?</strong></summary>

A runner is a machine or execution environment that performs pipeline jobs such as building, testing, scanning, and deploying software.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>4. Why is hardcoding secrets dangerous?</strong></summary>

Secrets written directly into source code may be exposed through repositories, Git history, logs, or unauthorized access. Attackers may use exposed credentials to access protected systems.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>5. What is the difference between environment variables and a secrets manager?</strong></summary>

Environment variables provide a way to supply configuration values to an application without hardcoding them. A secrets manager provides dedicated capabilities for securely storing, controlling, distributing, and auditing sensitive credentials.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>6. What is the difference between direct and transitive dependencies?</strong></summary>

A direct dependency is explicitly required by an application. A transitive dependency is required by another dependency. Both can introduce vulnerabilities.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>7. What is SCA?</strong></summary>

Software Composition Analysis examines an application's third-party components to identify vulnerable, outdated, or otherwise risky dependencies.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>8. What is an SBOM?</strong></summary>

A Software Bill of Materials is a structured inventory of the software components and dependencies included in an application.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>9. What is the difference between hashing and digital signing?</strong></summary>

Hashing produces a fixed-length representation of data and can help detect changes. Digital signing uses cryptographic keys to provide authenticity and integrity verification.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>10. What is build security?</strong></summary>

Build security protects the build environment, tools, scripts, dependencies, and generated artifacts from unauthorized access, compromise, and malicious modification.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>11. Why should production deployment permissions be restricted?</strong></summary>

If a deployment account or pipeline is compromised, excessive permissions may allow attackers to access or modify unrelated production resources. Least privilege limits the potential damage.
</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>12. What is the difference between blue-green and canary deployment?</strong></summary>

Blue-green deployment maintains two environments and switches traffic from the old version to the new version after validation. Canary deployment gradually releases the new version to a small percentage of users before expanding the rollout.
</details>

